#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078230 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec980fcb-97ed-5c60-94de-0521d9d7df27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Continuous Function Given the Function Expression"}] :question/id #uuid "df884ed9-c75e-5a38-abe2-425e01ee4aa7" :question/math-academy-id "e-432" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^5 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3, & x<0 \\\\ 2x+3, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}3\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x{|}_{-2}^{0}+({x}^{2}+3x){|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =[3(0)-3(-2)]+[({5}^{2}+3(5))-({0}^{2}+3(0))] \\\\  & =6+25+15 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078231 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "317454f0-3a04-5c1c-a451-2b8b2ec850ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-433" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/assets/ad45cbdcd126df931f1a35c60ca12b45d520a2a0a5cddf4fa012c0515d3024c7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x \\in [0,2)$ and the line segment for $x \\in [2,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{-A_2}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [0,2).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [2,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x =-A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078232 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "189d4a9a-fb2c-57b2-b9f6-9bec14fa1062" :question/math-academy-id "e-434" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/assets/36c6a1c84e0b0c32a5fd35fd422645f60340da6d205dd8cc31054cd558c2f5f9.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-4}^4 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of four parts: the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0],$ a line segment for $x\\in (0,2),$ a line segment for $x\\in (2,4],$ and the point at $x=2.$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+0+{{{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle with a radius of $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (0,2).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = -A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (2,4].$  The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi -2+2 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec980fcb-97ed-5c60-94de-0521d9d7df27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Continuous Function Given the Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b73702a1-67af-5e22-b65d-37f5dd1929fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289614} {:answer/id #uuid "942c0acf-063c-5f10-bf3b-6caeeb36c5bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "127" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289615} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef29137-7eda-5851-8e87-4646450e55ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "122" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289616} {:answer/id #uuid "5d6443d4-e468-5b46-9a8a-cef9c464313e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289617} {:answer/id #uuid "1a34c67c-e9c3-5a6f-b1c0-6fbfca30fdb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ef29137-7eda-5851-8e87-4646450e55ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "122" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289616} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ff15138-2413-54b3-b8ab-034b626452de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b6777ca-ff4c-593f-a186-002b5ace07e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-117662" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3x+5, & x<3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x+13, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}(3x+5)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}(\\frac{1}{3}x+13)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+5x){|}_{-1}^{3}+(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+13x){|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =[3\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}+5(3)-3\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}-5(-1)]+[\\frac{{9}^{2}}{6}+13(9)-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{6}-13(3)] \\\\  & =[12+20]+[12+78] \\\\  & =32+90 \\\\  & =122.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c87eeb99-3e4f-5c43-a2a9-9444183f192b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289669} {:answer/id #uuid "0f693360-1d6e-522c-bf4d-389e29b65467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289670} {:answer/id #uuid "b7336e5b-19d7-54c3-9d8b-d527be2ebcef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289671} {:answer/id #uuid "c93a060d-f9ad-5cad-802e-a562e8258701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289672} {:answer/id #uuid "f207fce9-e9d3-58ed-9d49-136a7c6a537e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7336e5b-19d7-54c3-9d8b-d527be2ebcef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289671} :answer-field/id #uuid "42712ed9-5a3c-5ef9-80d4-fbb4396ae6ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54902dd1-a8a7-5a9c-9e30-07d51d056c94" :question/math-academy-id "q-86167" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/assets/bac953cd2389ef36b415dcdce87730f244958ef0fe2f67911878c5cb3bfcafc6.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in (0,4],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [4,6].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)(4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [4,6].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =8+4-1 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdbdddce-6bb9-5d6f-bd08-be7557a8f000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293799} {:answer/id #uuid "4909127d-d3ef-50aa-9e10-a02ce28169eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293800} {:answer/id #uuid "97255af8-a483-51c4-80e3-0129d7413bd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293801} {:answer/id #uuid "77f0a833-75df-5102-8814-f868d63b9f87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293802} {:answer/id #uuid "cccd6aab-30ef-5d1e-b5c2-16056043112d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cccd6aab-30ef-5d1e-b5c2-16056043112d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293803} :answer-field/id #uuid "2014b57f-2aff-53dd-89c9-a9eda5d5e635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f31c4e7-003d-5a6c-9983-dff54f594d65" :question/math-academy-id "q-86174" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/assets/3e0eca1a04396c0c86bbf5c3e5a9b3ff7071eacfe3d8c4281549e70cda809e8c.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,3)$ and the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in (3,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,3).$ The corresponding region is a right triange, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (3,5].$ The corresponding region is a combination of an inverted quarter-circle of radius $2$ and a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =5+(4-\\pi ) \\\\  & =9-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
